WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.566
- [Instructor] So we've
added these physics materials

2
00:00:07.566 --> 00:00:09.200
to our player and our enemies

3
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.800
so that way when they collide
it creates this bouncy bump.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.966
Now we need to make it so that the enemy

5
00:00:17.966 --> 00:00:20.500
can actually chase the
player around the scene.

6
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.333
So we're going to use a script to do that.

7
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.000
In our project window,

8
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.133
we're going to go into our scripts folder,

9
00:00:32.666 --> 00:00:33.500
we're going to right-click

10
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.766
and create a new C sharp script.

11
00:00:35.766 --> 00:00:37.933
And for this we'll just call it enemy.

12
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:39.933
So this will hold all the behaviors

13
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:41.500
that the enemy should manage.

14
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.733
And we're going to make
sure we click and drag

15
00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:49.333
the enemy script onto the enemy.

16
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:52.533
And you can see it's been applied.

17
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.733
So now we're going to
go into our enemy script

18
00:00:57.733 --> 00:00:59.066
by opening it up.

19
00:00:59.066 --> 00:01:01.133
Now in our enemy script,

20
00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:03.566
let's think about some of
the things that we wanna do.

21
00:01:03.566 --> 00:01:06.833
So we're going to need a speed

22
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:08.233
to be able to make the enemy move.

23
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:10.966
So we'll make this another public

24
00:01:10.966 --> 00:01:13.566
so we can fix it in the editor.

25
00:01:13.566 --> 00:01:16.700
Float speed and we'll set this to,

26
00:01:17.533 --> 00:01:20.333
we'll set it a little slower,
so make it three for now.

27
00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:23.366
Another thing we need to do

28
00:01:23.366 --> 00:01:25.933
is because we're going to
try and move this object

29
00:01:25.933 --> 00:01:27.700
we need the rigid body component.

30
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.433
So we need a private rigid body.

31
00:01:34.366 --> 00:01:37.066
And we'll just call this enemy RB.

32
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.700
And now we need to
actually get the reference

33
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.466
of that rigid body on the object.

34
00:01:45.466 --> 00:01:48.666
So we use our enemy RB variable

35
00:01:50.166 --> 00:01:53.733
and we set that to the component

36
00:01:55.466 --> 00:01:59.100
of our rigid body on our enemy.

37
00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:02.400
Now we need to move this enemy.

38
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.633
To actually visualize what
we're trying to do here,

39
00:02:05.633 --> 00:02:09.600
we're trying to move the
enemy from their position

40
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.433
to our player's position.

41
00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.500
Now if we think about it in terms

42
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.533
of how we apply the force,

43
00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:18.866
since we're trying to move our enemy

44
00:02:18.866 --> 00:02:21.833
towards the player, we
should take our player's

45
00:02:21.833 --> 00:02:25.800
current position and then
we need to subtract it

46
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.766
from our enemy's position.

47
00:02:28.766 --> 00:02:32.733
So in fact, we actually need
a reference to our player.

48
00:02:32.733 --> 00:02:34.266
Since we don't have to find our player,

49
00:02:34.266 --> 00:02:36.400
we'll make this a private variable

50
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.000
and this will just be the game object.

51
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.733
And we'll just call it the player.

52
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.033
In our start method, we need
to find that actual player.

53
00:02:45.033 --> 00:02:47.600
So we'll use our player variable.

54
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.566
And we set that to the
game object in our scene,

55
00:02:52.466 --> 00:02:56.133
we find it by it's name,

56
00:02:56.133 --> 00:02:58.400
which in this case we named it player.

57
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.500
So now we have the reference to our player

58
00:03:01.966 --> 00:03:04.866
and so now we need to apply a force

59
00:03:04.866 --> 00:03:06.566
in the direction of our player.

60
00:03:06.566 --> 00:03:08.900
So we need our rigid body again

61
00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.266
and we add a force

62
00:03:16.866 --> 00:03:18.666
and how we're going to add this force

63
00:03:18.666 --> 00:03:23.333
is we're going to take
our player's position.

64
00:03:23.333 --> 00:03:27.800
So we need to use player
and we get the transform

65
00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.300
to get their current position.

66
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
And we're going to subtract by
our enemy's current position,

67
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.333
so we need our transform here.

68
00:03:39.666 --> 00:03:41.066
And we need the position.

69
00:03:42.233 --> 00:03:45.033
So now we know the direction
that we're trying to go

70
00:03:46.066 --> 00:03:48.300
by subtracting our player's position

71
00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.866
from the current enemy position.

72
00:03:50.866 --> 00:03:54.700
So that will give us the
vector three coordinates

73
00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.400
that we need to go towards at all times.

74
00:03:58.666 --> 00:04:01.566
In this case, we're
going to apply our speed

75
00:04:04.233 --> 00:04:07.133
so that way we know how fast
the enemy should be moving.

76
00:04:07.133 --> 00:04:09.033
One thing to consider is

77
00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:12.633
because this is actually a math equation,

78
00:04:12.633 --> 00:04:15.700
so our computer will read
this as a math equation.

79
00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:19.166
It will actually follow standard rules.

80
00:04:19.166 --> 00:04:23.066
So because transform.position
is being multiplied

81
00:04:23.066 --> 00:04:27.133
by speed, it'll actually do that first

82
00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:30.766
before it subtracts from the position.

83
00:04:30.766 --> 00:04:32.266
So that's not what we want to happen.

84
00:04:32.266 --> 00:04:35.433
We want to get the direction
that we're trying to go to

85
00:04:35.433 --> 00:04:37.666
before we apply the speed.

86
00:04:37.666 --> 00:04:40.166
So we use parentheses so
then that way make sure

87
00:04:40.166 --> 00:04:43.733
that first we're going to
subtract the player's position

88
00:04:43.733 --> 00:04:45.933
and our current position and then

89
00:04:45.933 --> 00:04:47.800
we're going to multiply that vector three

90
00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.766
by a certain speed.

91
00:04:49.766 --> 00:04:51.766
Now let's make sure we save.

92
00:04:51.766 --> 00:04:54.833
And then in unity, if we play our game,

93
00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.333
we should see the enemy is very,

94
00:04:59.333 --> 00:05:01.433
very aggressively rolling towards us.

95
00:05:01.433 --> 00:05:02.500
So that's good.

96
00:05:03.566 --> 00:05:05.266
Now one of the things that you might see

97
00:05:05.266 --> 00:05:07.733
is it was going very fast.

98
00:05:07.733 --> 00:05:09.566
Now the reason that happens is because

99
00:05:09.566 --> 00:05:13.333
as the player gets farther
away from the enemy,

100
00:05:13.333 --> 00:05:16.800
the length of that actual vector,

101
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.800
the distance between those
two positions becomes larger

102
00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.800
and so what it actually does, is it tries

103
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:26.300
to multiply it over a very
large amount of distance.

104
00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:29.700
So it actually increases
in force over time.

105
00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:32.066
We can actually use some code

106
00:05:32.066 --> 00:05:33.666
to prevent that from happening.

107
00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:37.666
So what we're gonna do is
we're going to actually

108
00:05:37.666 --> 00:05:40.300
create a standard strength

109
00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.800
so that way even if the enemy is

110
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.566
really far away from the player,

111
00:05:45.566 --> 00:05:48.566
it doesn't actually get
an increased magnitude,

112
00:05:48.566 --> 00:05:51.100
an increased strength
of it's force over time

113
00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.400
because the distance is larger.

114
00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:58.100
We can use a .normalized
after this vector three,

115
00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:59.166
so this will actually

116
00:05:59.166 --> 00:06:03.233
normalize the magnitude of this vector,

117
00:06:03.233 --> 00:06:04.533
so it doesn't matter how close

118
00:06:04.533 --> 00:06:06.666
or how far the enemy is from the player,

119
00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:09.333
it will always be coming
at the same speed.

120
00:06:09.333 --> 00:06:10.733
So if we save.

121
00:06:10.733 --> 00:06:14.300
So now in unity when we press play,

122
00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:18.100
we should see that the
enemy doesn't move at a

123
00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:23.100
very fast speed, it doesn't
accelerate really quickly

124
00:06:23.166 --> 00:06:25.466
when it runs into the player.

125
00:06:25.466 --> 00:06:26.966
Which is exactly what we want.

126
00:06:27.966 --> 00:06:29.366
So now it's your turn.

127
00:06:29.366 --> 00:06:31.666
You're going to go into your assets folder

128
00:06:31.666 --> 00:06:34.700
in your scripts and make an enemy script.

129
00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.766
Make sure you apply that enemy script

130
00:06:36.766 --> 00:06:38.900
to your enemy as a component.

131
00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:43.100
In your editor, you're going
to create three new variables,

132
00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:45.700
so we're gonna get a speed variable,

133
00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:48.500
we're going to get the rigid body,

134
00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.766
we're going to get a
reference to the player.

135
00:06:51.933 --> 00:06:54.100
In our start method, make sure that you

136
00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.700
get the component for that rigid body

137
00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.600
and make sure that you find that player

138
00:06:58.600 --> 00:06:59.766
in the game hierarchy.

139
00:07:01.033 --> 00:07:03.200
And then in our update method,

140
00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:06.866
you're going to add a force
to the enemy's rigid body.

141
00:07:06.866 --> 00:07:10.800
And you're going to get
the player's position,

142
00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:12.933
subtract our current position,

143
00:07:12.933 --> 00:07:15.433
so that we get a direction
we're trying to go to.

144
00:07:15.433 --> 00:07:18.100
Make sure that you put both
of those in parentheses.

145
00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:20.433
So that way it does that operation first.

146
00:07:20.433 --> 00:07:22.400
You're going to .normalized

147
00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.900
so that way we can actually
normalize that vector

148
00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:27.666
so it doesn't accelerate
at a very fast speed

149
00:07:27.666 --> 00:07:29.800
the farther the two objects are away.

150
00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.933
And then we're gonna multiply
it by the speed that we set.

151
00:07:32.933 --> 00:07:33.866
Now it's your turn.
